CAT = "pkpnmcAdult"; SEG = "ADU"
Q = [
("By which route is hepatitis C mainly passed from person to person?",
 "Blood, as with reused syringes", ["Coughing and sneezing", "Sharing food, plates and cups", "Bites from infected mosquitoes"],
 "Hepatitis C spreads through blood: reused syringes and needles, unscreened blood, unsterile dental or surgical tools, and shared razors. It is not spread by casual contact."),
("Which blood test result shows that a person currently has hepatitis B infection?",
 "Positive HBsAg", ["Positive anti-HBs only", "Raised serum amylase", "Low haemoglobin"],
 "Hepatitis B surface antigen in the blood means the virus is present now. Anti-HBs on its own shows immunity from vaccination or a past infection that has cleared."),
("A patient with dengue fever is in the critical phase. Which finding is a warning sign that needs urgent review?",
 "Persistent vomiting", ["A flushed face and red eyes", "Aching muscles and joints", "A fever on day one"],
 "Warning signs include persistent vomiting, abdominal pain, mucosal bleeding, lethargy, an enlarging liver and a rising haematocrit with falling platelets. They point to plasma leakage."),
("A patient with dengue fever asks for something for the fever and aches. Which drug is suitable?",
 "Paracetamol", ["Aspirin", "Ibuprofen", "Diclofenac sodium"],
 "Paracetamol is used for fever and pain in dengue. Aspirin and other anti-inflammatory drugs raise the risk of bleeding when platelets are low."),
("When is a patient with dengue most at risk of shock from plasma leakage?",
 "As the fever settles", ["In the first hours of fever", "Two weeks after recovery", "Only if the fever exceeds 41°C"],
 "The critical phase usually comes around days 3 to 7, as the fever falls. A falling temperature can hide worsening leakage, so the nurse keeps monitoring closely."),
("A patient in the third week of typhoid fever suddenly has severe abdominal pain and a rigid abdomen. What is suspected?",
 "Intestinal perforation", ["Normal recovery from the fever", "Simple indigestion", "Urinary retention from the bed rest"],
 "Typhoid ulcers in the small bowel can bleed or perforate, usually in the second or third week. A rigid, tender abdomen needs urgent surgical review."),
("Why should a person with diabetes rotate their insulin injection sites?",
 "To prevent fatty lumps under the skin", ["To make the insulin act more slowly", "To stop the pen needle from going blunt", "To reduce how much insulin is needed"],
 "Injecting the same spot causes lipohypertrophy, rubbery lumps where insulin is absorbed unpredictably. Rotating sites within an area prevents them."),
("What does an HbA1c test show?",
 "Average glucose over 3 months", ["Glucose at this exact moment", "Insulin still made by the pancreas", "Ketones in the last day"],
 "HbA1c measures glucose attached to haemoglobin, so it reflects the average over the life of red cells, roughly the last 2 to 3 months."),
("Which advice protects the feet of a person with diabetes?",
 "Check the feet every day", ["Walk barefoot to toughen the skin", "Soak the feet in hot water daily", "Cut corns with a razor blade"],
 "Nerve damage means injuries may not be felt. Daily checks, well-fitting shoes, never walking barefoot and seeing a nurse about corns prevent ulcers and amputations."),
("A nursing student has a documented full hepatitis B vaccine course. Her blood is negative for HBsAg and anti-HBc but positive for anti-HBs. What does this show?",
 "Immunity from vaccination", ["Current hepatitis B infection", "Chronic hepatitis B carrier state", "Hepatitis C infection"],
 "Anti-HBs indicates immunity. With HBsAg and anti-HBc both negative and a complete vaccine course recorded, the immunity comes from vaccination, not past infection."),
("A patient with diabetic ketoacidosis is breathing deeply and quickly. What is this breathing pattern called?",
 "Kussmaul breathing", ["Cheyne-Stokes breathing", "Biot's breathing", "Paradoxical breathing"],
 "Deep, rapid Kussmaul breathing blows off carbon dioxide to make up for the metabolic acidosis. The breath may smell fruity from acetone."),
("Which lifestyle change most helps to lower blood pressure in a patient with hypertension?",
 "Cutting down on salt", ["Drinking more tea", "Sleeping after meals", "Eating more white rice"],
 "Less salt, a healthy weight, regular exercise, not smoking and more fruit and vegetables all lower blood pressure. Salt reduction has one of the clearest effects."),
("A man with chest pain is due sublingual glyceryl trinitrate. Which drug taken in the past day makes it dangerous?",
 "Sildenafil", ["Paracetamol", "Omeprazole", "Metformin"],
 "Nitrates with sildenafil or similar drugs can cause a severe, sometimes fatal, drop in blood pressure. The nurse asks about these drugs before giving GTN."),
("A patient with heart failure is taught to weigh themselves every morning. What should they report?",
 "A gain of 2 kg over a few days", ["Any loss of 1 kg in a week", "No change in weight for a month", "A gain of 2 kg in a year"],
 "A quick rise in weight means fluid is building up, often before swelling or breathlessness are noticed, so treatment can be adjusted early."),
("A patient with heart failure says he needs three pillows to breathe at night. What is this called?",
 "Orthopnoea", ["Apnoea", "Tachypnoea", "Bradypnoea"],
 "Orthopnoea is breathlessness lying flat, eased by sitting up. Lying flat returns more blood to a failing heart and the lungs become congested."),
("A patient who has had a stroke has slurred speech and coughs when drinking. What is done before giving food or drink?",
 "A swallowing screen", ["A full meal to test hunger", "A glass of water to check", "A chest X-ray only"],
 "Stroke can weaken swallowing and food or fluid can enter the lungs, causing aspiration pneumonia. Nothing is given by mouth until swallowing has been screened."),
("Why should a patient rinse the mouth after using an inhaled steroid such as beclometasone?",
 "To prevent oral thrush", ["To make the drug work faster", "To stop a fast heartbeat", "To prevent tooth decay only"],
 "Steroid left in the mouth and throat can allow candida to grow and cause a hoarse voice. Rinsing and spitting, and using a spacer, reduce this."),
("A patient with COPD is taught pursed-lip breathing. How does it help?",
 "It keeps small airways open longer", ["It raises the carbon dioxide level", "It replaces the need for oxygen", "It clears the nose of mucus"],
 "Breathing out slowly through pursed lips creates back-pressure that holds the small airways open, so more trapped air leaves the lungs and the breathing rate slows."),
("In the DOTS approach to tuberculosis, what does directly observed treatment mean?",
 "Someone watches each dose being taken", ["The doctor sees the X-ray each week", "The patient takes tablets when they feel ill", "Doses are given only in hospital wards"],
 "A trained treatment supporter watches the patient swallow each dose, which helps them finish the full course and prevents drug resistance."),
("A patient has an arteriovenous fistula in the left arm for haemodialysis. What should the nurse avoid on that arm?",
 "Taking blood pressure", ["Feeling for a thrill", "Listening for a bruit", "Gentle hand exercises"],
 "Cuff pressure, tight clothing and needle sticks on the fistula arm can damage or clot it. A thrill felt and a bruit heard show the fistula is working."),
("Which organism causes most peptic ulcers?",
 "Helicobacter pylori", ["Escherichia coli bacteria", "Candida albicans", "Salmonella Typhi"],
 "H. pylori damages the stomach lining. Treatment combines antibiotics with an acid-reducing drug. The other common cause is anti-inflammatory painkillers."),
("A patient with cirrhosis has ascites. Which daily measurements track the fluid?",
 "Weight and abdominal girth", ["Pulse and pupil size", "Height, arm span and shoe size", "Temperature and peak flow"],
 "Weighing at the same time each day and measuring girth at the same marked level show whether fluid is building up or responding to diuretics and salt restriction."),
("Why are post-operative patients helped out of bed as early as their condition allows?",
 "To prevent clots and pneumonia", ["To shorten the wound scar", "To reduce the need for food", "To lower their body temperature fast"],
 "Early walking improves blood flow in the legs, which helps prevent deep vein thrombosis, and helps the lungs expand, which reduces atelectasis and pneumonia."),
("What is the purpose of an incentive spirometer after abdominal surgery?",
 "To encourage deep breaths", ["To measure blood oxygen", "To give nebulised drugs", "To check peak flow in asthma"],
 "The device rewards slow, deep breaths, which open collapsed alveoli and help prevent atelectasis after surgery that makes breathing painful."),
("A patient with suspected appendicitis asks for a hot water bottle for the pain. What should the nurse say?",
 "Avoid heat on the abdomen", ["Use it on the right side only", "Heat is best for this pain", "Use it for one hour at most"],
 "Heat may speed inflammation and could hasten rupture of an inflamed appendix. The patient is kept nil by mouth and assessed for surgery."),
("Using the rule of nines, an adult has burns to both whole arms and the perineum. What percentage of body surface is burned?",
 "19 percent", ["10 percent", "18 percent", "27 percent"],
 "In an adult each whole arm is 9 percent and the perineum 1 percent, so 9 plus 9 plus 1 is 19 percent."),
("Which checks make up neurovascular observations of a limb in a new plaster cast?",
 "Colour, warmth, pulses, movement, feeling", ["Height, weight and blood pressure", "Pupils, speech and grip of the other hand", "Temperature, urine output and appetite"],
 "Checking colour, warmth, capillary refill, pulses, movement and sensation below the cast picks up swelling that is cutting off blood supply or pressing on nerves."),
("After thyroid surgery a patient's voice becomes hoarse and weak. Which nerve may have been injured?",
 "Recurrent laryngeal nerve", ["Facial nerve", "Phrenic nerve", "Vagus nerve branch to the heart"],
 "The recurrent laryngeal nerves run close to the thyroid and supply the vocal cords. Damage can weaken the voice or cause hoarseness, so the voice is checked after surgery."),
("Which sign is typical of an overactive thyroid?",
 "Heat intolerance", ["Weight gain", "Slow pulse", "Dry, coarse hair and cold skin"],
 "Hyperthyroidism speeds up metabolism, causing heat intolerance, weight loss, a fast pulse, tremor and anxiety. The other signs suggest an underactive thyroid."),
("A patient starting oral iron tablets should be told to expect which change?",
 "Dark or black stools", ["Orange-red urine", "Blue-tinged lips", "Loss of taste for sweet foods"],
 "Unabsorbed iron turns stools dark green or black, which is harmless. Black tarry stools with weakness or dizziness may still mean bleeding and are reported."),
("During a heatwave, a labourer is confused, with a temperature of 41°C and hot, dry skin. What is the nursing priority?",
 "Cool the body rapidly", ["Give paracetamol and wait", "Cover him with a blanket", "Give him strong tea"],
 "This is heatstroke, a medical emergency. Rapid cooling with water, fanning and ice packs in the armpits and groin lowers the core temperature. Paracetamol does not work in heatstroke."),
("A labourer has a deep, dirty wound from a rusty metal sheet. What must the nurse check?",
 "His tetanus vaccination status", ["His blood group", "His eyesight", "His cholesterol and lipid levels"],
 "Tetanus spores live in soil and dirt. A deep, dirty wound in someone not fully vaccinated needs a tetanus booster and, if needed, tetanus immunoglobulin."),
("A patient with chronic kidney disease is advised to limit which mineral found in bananas and dried fruit?",
 "Potassium", ["Iron", "Iodine", "Vitamin B12"],
 "Failing kidneys cannot remove potassium well, and high levels can cause heart rhythm problems. Bananas, dates, dried apricots and potatoes are rich in potassium."),
("Which sign suggests a patient is developing deep vein thrombosis in the leg?",
 "A swollen, warm, painful calf", ["A cold, pale foot with no pain", "Itching between the toes", "A rash on both shins"],
 "DVT typically causes one-sided calf swelling, warmth, redness and pain. The leg should not be massaged because a clot could break off and travel to the lungs."),
("Which advice helps prevent constipation in a patient who is less mobile?",
 "More fluid and fibre", ["Less water in the day", "Eating only white bread", "Staying in bed all day"],
 "Fluids, fibre from vegetables, pulses and whole grains, and as much activity as possible keep stools soft and the bowel moving."),
("What is the main aim of nursing care for a patient with a fractured neck of femur awaiting surgery?",
 "Pain relief and pressure care", ["Encourage walking on the leg", "Apply heat to the hip", "Keep the patient nil by mouth for days"],
 "These patients, often elderly, need good pain control, careful positioning, pressure care and monitoring while they wait for surgery, which is done as soon as it is safe."),
]
